WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.360
Take a simple cell's best bar and slide it sideways, off the ON strip and onto an

2
00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:10.660
OFF strip. What happens to the sum?

3
00:00:16.683 --> 00:00:19.103
It falls past zero and goes negative.

4
00:00:20.023 --> 00:00:22.523
This cell goes from firing hard to falling silent.

5
00:00:23.567 --> 00:00:25.887
Hubel and Wiesel's complex cells did.

6
00:00:26.707 --> 00:00:29.867
A correctly oriented bar drove them over much of the field.

7
00:00:30.850 --> 00:00:33.050
A bar at the wrong angle still did little.

8
00:00:33.910 --> 00:00:38.410
In many complex cells that answered spots, turning a spot on and turning it off

9
00:00:38.410 --> 00:00:43.350
excited the same places, unlike a simple cell's separate ON and OFF subregions.

10
00:00:44.190 --> 00:00:46.790
Other complex cells did not answer small spots.

11
00:00:47.833 --> 00:00:49.593
One sum cannot do that.

12
00:00:50.413 --> 00:00:53.033
Swap a bright bar for a dark one, and the sum flips sign.

13
00:00:54.017 --> 00:00:56.177
Their proposal applies the rule twice.

14
00:00:57.417 --> 00:01:01.217
Several simple cells share one angle, with fields at slightly different places.

15
00:01:02.367 --> 00:01:04.567
All of them excite one complex cell.

16
00:01:05.407 --> 00:01:09.347
Where their responses overlap enough, at least one of them answers as the bar

17
00:01:09.347 --> 00:01:10.347
shifts.

18
00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:19.020
So the complex cell keeps its preferred angle and gains tolerance of position.

19
00:01:20.133 --> 00:01:22.053
This second step is still debated.

20
00:01:22.973 --> 00:01:27.513
Some complex cells receive direct geniculate input, and circuits within cortex

21
00:01:27.513 --> 00:01:28.513
contribute.

22
00:01:29.183 --> 00:01:34.323
In cat and monkey, an electrode going straight down through cortex meets cells that

23
00:01:34.323 --> 00:01:35.343
tend to share an angle.

24
00:01:36.103 --> 00:01:37.103
That is a column.

25
00:01:38.217 --> 00:01:41.597
Does a cell need neighbours that agree with it in order to be tuned?

26
00:01:47.667 --> 00:01:53.127
No. Imaging rat visual cortex cell by cell found sharply tuned cells.

27
00:01:53.967 --> 00:01:55.967
Their neighbours preferred unrelated angles.

28
00:01:57.017 --> 00:01:59.057
This layout is called salt-and-pepper.

29
00:01:59.917 --> 00:02:01.197
Tuning does not need a map.

30
00:02:02.117 --> 00:02:04.377
So tuning does not need neighbours that agree.

31
00:02:05.277 --> 00:02:08.017
How each cell gets its preference is a separate question.

32
00:02:09.017 --> 00:02:10.817
So keep the rule and its limit.

33
00:02:11.657 --> 00:02:16.157
A simple cell adds up the light in its field, and the field's layout largely sets

34
00:02:16.157 --> 00:02:19.797
its preference. A complex cell needs more than one sum.
